course: biology
lesson: 139
title: Peppa's friends as real animals: sheep, ponies, parrots, gazelles, kangaroos
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Peppa's friends are cartoon animals that talk and go to school, but each one is based on a real animal with its own clever tricks.
explain: Look a sheep in the eye and you will see a long sideways slit for a pupil, giving it a wide view of the ground all around so it can spot a fox while it grazes.
explain: A real parrot copies words the way a wild parrot copies its flock, and it climbs its cage using its beak as a third foot.
explain: Rebecca Rabbit living in a burrow is the one that is true to life, while Danny Dog's grandad and every other dog, tiny or huge, all belong to one kind of animal that came from wolves.
fact: Real sheep live in flocks, stay close to their friends and get stressed when alone, because a lone sheep is easy for a fox or dog to catch.
fact: In experiments sheep learned to recognise the faces of other sheep and of some famous people from photos.
fact: Sheep and goats have long rectangular pupils lying sideways; when they bend to graze their eyes turn so the pupil stays level, and they can see almost all the way around without moving their heads.
fact: Most farm sheep grow wool that never stops, so they must be shorn at least once a year, while wild sheep shed their coats every spring; an Australian merino that escaped shearing for years carried about 41 kilograms of fleece.
fact: Ponies are small horses, usually under about 1.47 metres at the shoulder, often from cold hilly places like Shetland, Wales or Dartmoor, with short legs and thick coats for wind and rain.
fact: For its size a Shetland pony is one of the strongest horses and once pulled carts in coal mines.
fact: Horses and ponies graze up to about 16 hours a day, a little at a time, because their stomachs are small, and they cannot vomit, so a bad meal can make them very ill.
fact: Many parrots copy words and sounds such as phones and doorbells, the way wild parrots copy their flock's calls; an African grey called Alex learned to name over 50 objects, colours and shapes.
fact: Parrots use their strong curved beaks to crack nuts and as a third foot for climbing, and they hold food in one foot, many being left-footed or right-footed.
fact: Gazelles are slim, fast antelopes of Africa and Asia that escape cheetahs and lions by running and dodging, and they sometimes bounce on stiff legs, called pronking or stotting, which may tell a hunter «I am fit, don't chase me».
fact: Kangaroos hop on big back legs whose tendons spring back like rubber bands, and they use their thick tail for balance and as a fifth leg when moving slowly, and mothers carry their babies, called joeys, in a pouch.
fact: Kangaroos find walking backwards very hard, and a popular story says that is why they are on Australia's coat of arms, as a sign of going forward.
fact: Wild rabbits really live in burrows joined into big tunnel systems called warrens, with many exits for escape; some warrens have been used for over a hundred years.
fact: All pet dogs, from tiny chihuahuas to great danes, are one kind of animal, bred by people for hunting, herding, guarding and company, and they descend from wolves tamed at least 15,000 years ago.
exercise: Why do real sheep stay together in a flock? | A lone sheep is easy for a fox or dog to catch | Sheep cannot see very well when they are on their own | The farmer ties them together | Sheep need to keep each other's wool clean
exercise: What did sheep learn in face experiments? | To recognise other sheep and some famous people from photos | To read their own names on the sign hanging by the gate of the field | To count to a hundred | To draw faces with their hooves
exercise: What shape is a sheep's pupil? | A long sideways rectangle | A round circle like ours | A tall up-and-down slit like a cat's | A star shape
exercise: How does a sideways pupil help a grazing sheep? | It gives a wide view all around to spot predators | It helps it see all the colours of the flowers very brightly | It helps it see far into the sky | It lets it see in total darkness
exercise: Why must most farm sheep be shorn? | Their wool never stops growing | Shearing keeps them from flying away | Their wool turns into hair if left | Wool makes them too cold in winter
exercise: What is a pony? | A small horse, usually under about 1.47 metres at the shoulder | A baby horse that will later grow up into a big, tall horse like its mother | A kind of donkey | A cross between a horse and a goat
exercise: Why do many ponies have short legs and thick coats? | They come from cold, windy, hilly places | They live in hot, sandy deserts with little water | They were bred only for racing | They live mostly in water
exercise: What job did Shetland ponies once do? | Pull carts in coal mines | Carry knights into battle | Pull racing chariots | Guard sheep from wolves
exercise: How long can a pony graze in a day? | Up to about 16 hours, a little at a time | About 10 minutes, all at once in the morning | Only at night for 1 hour | It eats only once a week
exercise: Why must horse owners be careful what they feed? | Horses cannot vomit, so a bad meal can make them very ill | Horses only eat once a year, so each meal must be just right | Horses are allergic to grass | Horses spit out everything new
exercise: Why does a pet parrot copy its family's words? | Wild parrots copy their flock, and it treats the family as its flock | It understands every single word we say, just like a person does at school | It is trying to annoy people | It has been given a speaking pill
exercise: What did Alex the African grey parrot learn? | To name over 50 objects, colours and shapes | To read whole books aloud to its owner at night | To fly a plane | To sing every song on the radio
exercise: How does a parrot use its beak when climbing? | As a third foot for gripping | As a hammer to break the bars | As a nose for smelling the way | It never uses its beak for climbing
exercise: What are gazelles? | Slim, fast antelopes of Africa and Asia | Small red deer from the forests of Europe | A kind of goat from the mountains | Baby giraffes
exercise: Why might a gazelle bounce high on stiff legs when it sees a cheetah? | To show it is fit and not worth chasing | To get a better look at the sky for eagles | Because it has hurt its legs | To dance for other gazelles
exercise: What does a kangaroo's thick tail do? | Helps it balance and works as a fifth leg when moving slowly | Stores water for long trips across the desert, like the hump of a camel | Carries its baby | Swats away flies only
exercise: What are joined rabbit burrows called? | A warren | A sett | A drey | A lodge
exercise: Are a chihuahua and a great dane different kinds of animal? | No, all pet dogs are one kind of animal | Yes, they are different species | Yes, one is a wolf and one is a dog | No, they are both kinds of fox from different countries
test: «Kylie, her mum n her little bro all hopping, how many big back legs is that» | 6 | 12 | 3 | 9
test: «flock of 9 sheep, wait no, 10, how many eyes with those sideways slits» | 20 | 18 | 10 | 40
test: «Polly repeats grandpa so parrots understand everything we say» What is right? | Parrots copy sounds like a flock call; some, like Alex, learned many names | Yes, every parrot understands all our words and answers us on purpose, like a person | No, parrots cannot copy any sounds | Yes, parrots think in English
test: In Winnie the Pooh, Kanga carries baby Roo in her pouch. Which part is true to life? | Kangaroo mothers really carry their young in a pouch | Kangaroo fathers carry the babies in a pouch on their backs | Kangaroos keep babies in a nest of leaves | Kangaroo babies ride on their mothers' tails
test: «Rebecca Rabbit lives in a hole under a hill, that's made up» What is right? | True to life: wild rabbits live in burrows joined into warrens | Made up: rabbits live in trees | Made up: rabbits live in round nests on top of the long grass, like birds | Made up: rabbits sleep in caves
test: «sheep alone in the field bleating like mad, drama queen» Why is it upset? | Sheep are flock animals, and a lone sheep feels unsafe | It is hungry for meat and wants the farmer to come and feed it some | It wants to be shorn | It is singing to the farmer
test: What would happen to a farm sheep that was never shorn for years? | Its fleece would keep growing very heavy and hot | Its wool would just drop off by itself every spring | It would turn into a goat | Nothing, wool stops growing after a year
test: «gazelle's jumping up and down in front of the cheetah, it's mental» What may it be doing? | Showing it is fit, so the cheetah does not bother chasing | Trying to scare the cheetah away by running at it and biting its legs | Asking the cheetah to play | It has stepped on a thorn
test: «gave the pony a massive bucket of grain, it'll just throw up if it's too much» What is right? | Horses cannot vomit, so a bad meal can make them very ill | Yes, ponies throw up easily, just like dogs and cats do | Yes, and then eat it again | No, ponies cannot eat grain at all
test: «Danny Dog's grandad and a tiny chihuahua can't be the same animal» What is right? | They are: all dogs are one kind, bred into many shapes from wolves | Right, big dogs come from bears and small dogs come from foxes, it is well known | Right, small dogs come from foxes | Right, they are a dog and a wolf
test: «parrot's chewing the cage bars to climb, it's trying to escape» What is it most likely doing? | Using its beak as a third foot to climb | Sharpening its beak to bite people | Trying to eat the metal | Building a nest out of bars
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the shape and function of the pupil of the sheep. | It is a horizontal rectangle affording a wide view of the surrounding ground | It is circular and adapted chiefly for the perception of colour | It is a vertical slit, like the eye of a cat, adapted for hunting at night | It is star-shaped and adapted for looking upward
exam: Identify the principal reason for which most domestic sheep require shearing at least annually. | Their wool continues to grow without being shed | Shearing prevents the animals from overheating in winter | Their wool turns to hair if it is not removed | Shearing is required by law for reasons of appearance
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pony? | It is a horse of small stature, frequently originating in cold and hilly regions | It is the juvenile form of the full-sized horse, which attains its full stature in a few years | It is a variety of donkey | It is the offspring of a horse and a goat
exam: Ascertain the behaviour designated as pronking or stotting in the gazelle. | Leaping high upon stiff legs, which may signal to a predator that pursuit would be futile | Lying motionless in the grass in order to escape notice | Uttering a loud and repeated cry in order to summon the remainder of the herd to its aid | Running in circles in order to confuse the predator
